{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Announcement timing, and what else needs the same treatment</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      <code>aria-live</code> isn't limited to red error text — any content that updates without a full page navigation (a "saved" confirmation, a cart item count, a search results count) needs the same consideration: will a screen reader user know it changed at all if they're not currently focused there?
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Clearing and rewriting the exact same error text back-to-back can fail to re-announce in some screen reader/browser pairings, since there's technically no textual diff to notice — a common workaround is briefly clearing the live region to an empty string, then setting the real message on the next frame, forcing a genuine mutation the live region has to react to.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Live regions and <code>role="alert"</code> both only work reliably if the element carrying the attribute is present in the DOM before the content change — inserting a brand-new element with <code>aria-live="polite"</code> already containing the error text, versus updating an existing empty live region, are not equivalent across every screen reader/browser combination; some announce the former inconsistently because the live-region registration and the content mutation land in the same paint. Render the empty live-region container up front, and only ever mutate its contents afterward — verified with an actual screen reader (VoiceOver + Safari, or NVDA + Firefox), not just an automated scan, since automated tools catch the missing attribute but can't confirm the announcement timing actually works.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

